div.p:
            5. The Third Common Assertion. —
            I say therefore thirdly: God, according to the absolute ratio of his Deity and essence,
            is the primary object of faith.
            This is, as I believe, the common opinion of the theologians,
            of St. Thomas here, and
            Part 1, q. 1, art. 7, and
            q. 14 of De Veritate, art. 8, and
            in Book 3, dist. 24, art. 1;
            and the same is held by Scotus, dist. 23, single question;
            Durandus, dist. 24, question 1;
            Capreolus, question 4 of the Prologue, art. 2;
            Albert and others, in Book 3, dist. 23;
            Marsilius also holds this, in Book 1, q. 2,
            where he reports various opinions;
            also held by Henry of Ghent, Quodlibet 12, q. 1;
            Alexander of Hales, Part 3, q. 68, member 7, final article.
            Now, in order to explain and prove this,
            we must distinguish a twofold formal ratio,
            as is commonly held regarding the objects of sciences or habits:
            one is the formal ratio under which,
            and this is that which moves toward knowledge of the object,
            or constitutes it in the ratio of the knowable;
            the other is the ratio which is primarily intended, or known in the subject.
            Here, then, we are not speaking of the ratio under which,
            for that pertains to the following disputation,
            but of the ratio which is principally intended by faith;
            and in this mode we say that God himself, insofar as he is God,
            is the primary scope of faith.
            This seems sufficiently proved from those words of Paul:
            one approaching God must believe that he is;
            for since God's being is his substance and essence,
            to know that God is, is the same as to know that God is the true God,
            and thus to have true divinity;
            therefore this is the primary object which faith intends.
            And so Christ was saying, John 14:
            believe in God, and believe also in me;
            because that first is the foundation of the rest;
            there is also that passage, John 17:
            this is eternal life, that they know you;
            therefore this absolute knowledge of God is what is intended by faith.
            Likewise, it is the primary and essential object

of our beatitude and the vision of God;
            therefore also of faith:
            both because vision succeeds faith, as I said;
            and also because faith and vision have the same primary object,
            with only the ratio of clear and obscure varying.
            Finally, whatever is revealed through faith is ordered to this end,
            that God, according to himself and according to his absolute ratio,
            may be more perfectly known, according to the capacity of the present state;
            therefore God, according to the aforesaid absolute ratio of Deity,
            is the subject of faith.

1. The First Assertion, Against Which Palude's Statement Militates. — That Statement Appears Contrary to Faith. —
First, it must be certain that not only God but also created things are the material subject of faith,
because many things concerning creatures are believed through faith.
Hence I am greatly surprised how Palude, in Book 3, dist. 24, q. 4, concl. 2,
could have said that there is no proposition believed through infused faith
whose subject is a creature, however much the predicate may be, if not God.
From this position it openly follows, against our assertion,
that nothing created is a subject of faith;
for, as I explained above, that is called a subject of faith
about which something is predicated that is believed through infused faith;
if therefore propositions about a created subject are not believed through infused faith,
there is no created subject of faith.
Yet that position appears not only improbable
but openly contrary to faith,


for this proposition, Abraham begat Isaac,
must be believed through infused faith, since it is expressed in Scripture;
and likewise this one: Blessed Mary was a perpetual Virgin;
and similar propositions in which the subjects are created things.
There can therefore be no doubt about the assertion stated.

2. The Second Assertion. —
Second, it must be said that God is not the adequate subject, or, as I would put it, the direct object of faith,
but that this is rather any being revealable by God through obscure revelation.
This is gathered from St. Thomas here, art. 1,
and the said question 15 of De Veritate, art. 8;
it is taught by Scotus, in Book 3, dist. 23, q. unica;
and by Gabriel and others commonly, partly there and partly in dist. 14;
and by Antoninus, Part 3, tit. 8, c. 4;
and by Abulensis, on Matthew 22, q. 301.
And it is easily proved, because first of all, for something to be comprehended under the object of faith,
it is necessary that something about it can be believed through faith,
since only that is contained under the matter or subject of a habit
about which the habit can operate;
but faith cannot operate except concerning a subject revealed by God,
as will be evident from what will be said below about the formal aspect of the object of faith;
therefore, for something to be contained under the object of faith,
it is necessary that it be revealable by God.
And therefore a lie, insofar as it is such, cannot be an object of faith,
because it is not revealable by God;
I say insofar as it is such, that is, insofar as it contains falsehood
in what I would call the exercised act;
for if a lie is taken insofar as something true can be affirmed of it,
in that way it can also be comprehended among the subjects of faith;
for this proposition, lying is a sin, is of faith;
but this very thing is revealable by God;
so this is required in the first place.
That this suffices is proved by the contrary argument:
for every matter that can be informed by the formal objective aspect of some habit
belongs to the material object of that habit and is comprehended under the adequate object;
but whatever can be revealed by God can be informed by divine testimony,
which is the formal object of faith;
therefore all of this is comprehended under the adequate subject of faith;
but it is clear that not only God but also many created things are revealed by God;
therefore God alone is not the adequate direct object of faith;
therefore no other description can be assigned except being revealable —
by a revelation, namely, that is obscure and suited to faith;
for no other common aspect can be assigned to all these subjects.

1. The First Assertion. — Explained and Proved. —
First, it is certain that the object which faith immediately believes is something complex;
for it is a catholic truth to which our intellect gives assent by composing or dividing.
So holds St. Thomas here, art. 2,
and the other scholastics in Book 3, dist. 23 and

24, who use various distinctions to explain this matter;
but there is no need to dwell on what is clear.
It must therefore be said that the complexity we are treating
is formally and properly in the formal conceptions of the mind,
and from there is shared by the signs of words or writings by which those concepts are expressed;
whereas to things or subjects it is attributed only by extrinsic denomination from the concepts of the mind;
since therefore we do not believe through faith except through complex concepts,
by composing and dividing,
faith is said to be immediately concerned with a complex object.
And therefore St. Thomas, in the said art. 2,
said that the things or truths believed, as they are in themselves, are simple;
but as they are in us through faith, they are complex —
by denomination from our acts.
But when he says that such things are simple in themselves,
this must not be understood universally of a simplicity that excludes all composition;
for this is proper to those truths that pertain to the divinity —
for to this complex proposition, God is three and one,
there corresponds a thing that is utterly simple in itself;
but truths of faith that contain something created admit real composition in the object itself;
yet it is called simple insofar as it is opposed to composition of the mind.
Hence it can also be noted in passing that this complexity befalls faith
and its object as it exists in us,
for in angels it can be found without it.

4. A Doubt about the Order of the Object in the Mode of the Credible. — First Opinion, Denying It. — First Foundation of that Opinion. — Second. — Third. — The principal difficulty of this passage concerns these objects considered in the mode of the credible, and especially regarding the order of dependence, for in the other respects the matter is clear; for it is certain that all these objects, as credible through faith, are equal in certainty, infallibility, and the authority on account of which they are believed. Regarding the order of dependence, however, there is a doubt whether one credible thing is prior to another, and thus whether there is some first credible thing upon which the rest depend in the mode of the credible. On this doubt, the first opinion simply denies that any such order is given among the credible things of faith. Cited in favor of this opinion are all the authors who say that all truths of faith immediately rest, as regards their credibility, upon divine authority, such as Cajetan and other modern authors, II-II, q.4, a.1; William of Auxerre, in the Prologue of the whole Summa; Major, in book 3, distinction 23, question 9, and others there. But these authors by virtue of that principle do not commit themselves to this opinion; for, as we shall see, it is not inconsistent that all truths of faith are immediately credible by divine authority, and yet that one is prior to another in the mode of the credible. More clearly pointing to this opinion is Scotus, in book 3, distinction 23, question 2, toward the end; for he says that faith immediately assents to all things and not to one on account of another, as if by syllogistic reasoning; and Gabriel, question 2 holds the same there; and Capreolus, article 3, reply to the third objection, against the second conclusion,
saying that one who believes God to be triune and one, because God said so, does not elicit two assents but one, by which he believes both that God is triune and that God said this — just as Thomas said in question 2, article 2 that it is the same act by which we believe God and believe in God. The foundation of this opinion is that, if among the credible things through faith, insofar as they are such, this order were given, faith would use reasoning in believing: but the consequent is false; therefore. The sequence is proved because the order of dependence among propositions as regards assent is grounded in this: that the assent to one is the reason for assenting to another, and this is reasoning. The falsity of the consequent, moreover, is proved because faith is grounded solely in divine authority, not in human reasoning; otherwise it would have no greater certainty than human reasoning or the force of inference, which cannot be said, as will be clear below. Secondly, I argue in this way: divine revelation of itself regards immediately and without order the propositions that are or can be believed through faith; therefore such propositions have no order or dependence among themselves in the mode of the credible. The consequence is evident, because all those propositions are constituted in the mode of the credible through divine revelation. The antecedent is proved because, since God freely reveals, he can reveal any truth and not another; and therefore, whatever truth is assigned as first in the order of the credible, God could reveal others and not that one, and thus it is shown not to be first, speaking per se and from the intrinsic necessity of faith. Thirdly, if there were any order among the propositions of faith, it would be chiefly between those that contain the formal reason for believing and the others that are merely believed; but not even between those is there an order; therefore neither between any others. The major is clarified: among the propositions of faith, some signify the formal reason for believing, such as: God is the first truth who can neither be deceived nor deceive, and: God said this; while others are merely believed, such as: Christ rose, and we shall rise. Now if these latter propositions are compared among themselves, they have no order in the mode of the credible; for, though in reality one follows from another, and we sometimes therefore argue from one to the other, as Paul argues in Romans 8: If children, then heirs; and 1 Corinthians 15: If Christ rose, then we also shall rise;
nevertheless that argumentation is not per se necessary for believing, nor is it the reason for believing; for faith would equally believe both propositions even if one did not follow from the other, and simple believers who do not know how to argue believe both equally firmly. In these propositions, then, it is manifest that there is no order of prior and posterior among them insofar as they are credible; and thus the major proposition is established, because if there is any order, it can only be conceived between the material and the formal. Now the minor is proved: those propositions by which we signify the formal reason for believing are not the reason for believing the others as believed propositions; rather the reason for believing is only the first truth itself, as revealing any proposition; and the first truth thus revealing is not something complex, but is God himself, as testifying or revealing what is believed; therefore, by virtue of this formal reason, there is no order among the propositions believed, but only between the material and the formal object of faith, insofar as the formal object is that by which we believe, and not insofar as it is itself something believed.

12. Reply to the First Foundation in Number 4. — To the first argument of the previous opinion,
which concerned reasoning in faith,
some do not hesitate to grant that faith assents by means of reasoning;
but I do not settle this now, for I shall speak of it
below, in disputation 6, section 4, treating the act of faith;
for now, however, an easy reply can be given by denying the sequence.
For it is one thing for the intellect to need the composition of several propositions
and a passage (so to speak) from one to another in order to conceive the assent of faith;
it is another thing to assent through faith by virtue of reasoning.
The first is true, and especially with respect to those propositions
that are not the reason for believing and do not contain it within themselves;
and this alone is what that first argument proves —
indeed below we shall show the same to be necessary in its own way
for the sufficient proposition of faith.
The second, however, is not necessary;
for to assent by virtue of reasoning is to be grounded in it,
such that the per se cause of assenting is the connection of the extremes with the middle;
but it is not necessary that faith assent in this way;
rather, once the intellect conceives that the medium of faith is sufficiently applied to the given object,
it simply believes it on account of the authority of God,
without the proper and per se foundation of

reasoning, or of the necessity of inference or consequence.

6. The Assertion Is Then Proved by Reason. — From these things, therefore,



the conclusion remains sufficiently proved by authority;
but its reason is the same as what has already been touched upon
in declaring what moved the Apostles to compose a symbol,
namely, that the faithful might have a brief form for professing the faith;
for the Apostle says in Rom. 10:
With the heart one believes unto righteousness,
and with the mouth confession — namely of faith — is made unto salvation;
therefore, lest simple faithful err in a confession of this kind,
it was necessary to hand down a symbol to them,
so that, with fixed and certain words, as the cited Fathers say,
they might make this confession;
likewise so that through this uniformity it might be established
that the faith of the entire Catholic Church is one,
as Epiphanius, in the Ancoratus, at the end said;
finally, so that the faithful might always have at hand
the matter which it is necessary to believe explicitly.

7. Second Assertion. — Proved First from Hebrews 11. —
Proved Second from John 14. — Proved Third by Reason. —
Hence I say second: this matter of faith is sufficiently contained in the Symbol.
This is proved briefly: first, with St. Thomas,
from that text in Hebrews 11:
Faith is the substance of things hoped for;
for in the Symbol all things are contained that pertain to this substance,
for there God is taught, who is the object of the beatitude we hope for;
Christ is also handed down, who is the way through which we tend toward it;
and concerning both, all the substantial and most necessary mysteries are taught;
and finally the present Church is set forth, within which alone salvation can be hoped for;
likewise eternal life and the resurrection are set forth, which we hope for,
and finally the future judgment is not omitted,
and consequently the eternal punishment prepared for the wicked is also intimated,
because fear too is necessary, lest hope pass into presumption;
therefore the Symbol most sufficiently embraces the matter of faith.
From this it can also be confirmed by the words of Christ,
John 14:
This is eternal life, that they may know you,
the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent,
together with the words of Augustine, sermon 181 on the Seasons:
The Symbol is brief in words but great in mysteries;
for whatever has been handed down in the Scriptures concerning God and Christ
is contained in the symbol.
Finally this is most aptly confirmed from the necessity of explicit faith;
because in the Symbol is contained whatever is necessary or must be believed explicitly.
Whence Pope Leo above
says that this rule suffices for the instruction of the faithful,
for it contains everything that God willed no one in the Church to be ignorant of.

3. First Assertion. — Proved from Paul. — Likewise from the Fathers. — Likewise from Reason. —
With these points established, it must be said first that faith with regard to the substance
of things to be believed has always been the same from the beginning of the human race until now.
So teaches St. Thomas here, article 7;
Hugh of St. Victor, Book 1 on the Sacraments, part 10, chapter 4 and following;
the Master, in Book 3, distinction 25, where the Doctors commonly [agree];
Bonaventure, article 2, question 1;
Albert, article 1, to the second;
Scotus,
Durandus,
Richard, and others, question 1;
and Alensis, third part, question sixty-nine.
And it is clearly proved from Paul, in Hebrews 11,
where, when he had said:
Without faith it is impossible to please God;
for one who approaches God must believe that he exists and that he is a rewarder;
these two things contain by force the whole substance of the matter of faith.
Likewise in the same place he defines
faith as the substance of things hoped for;
but the things hoped for have always been the same in substance,
namely, eternal beatitude and those things that pertain to it.
Whence Paul adds in the same place:
All these died in faith, not having received the promises,
but seeing them afar off.
Furthermore, 2 Corinthians 4:
Having (he says) the same spirit of faith;
and he is speaking of the ancient Fathers.
Also relevant is that of Acts 4:
There is no other name under heaven given to men
by which we must be saved;
whence it is established that faith in Christ was always and ever necessary for salvation,
as Paul also expounded in Acts 17,
Romans 3,
and Hebrews 9;
and as St. Gregory expounded in homily seventeen on Ezekiel,
applying that of Mark 11:
And those who went before and those who followed
all cried out: Hosanna to the son of David.
Moreover, this is the common consensus of the Fathers,
as is evident from Irenaeus,



book four, chapter thirteen;
Eusebius, book one of the History, chapter four,
where on this ground he says that all the faithful from the beginning of the world
could be called Christians;
likewise Epiphanius, heresy 66;
more extensively Augustine, tractate 45 on John:
"The times have changed, not the faith" —
and he is speaking of faith with respect to its matter —
whence he adds: "The sound of the word has changed — 'he will come' or 'he has come' —
yet the same faith joins both."
He has similar things in book eighteen of the City of God,
chapter forty-seven,
and in sermon eight on the Seasons:
One (he says) is the faith of the New and Old Testament;
likewise Letter 157 to Optatus;
at length in book nineteen against Faustus, chapter fourteen and following;
Jerome, on Galatians 2 and 3;
Chrysostom and others.
The argument from reason is that there has always been the same Church among wayfaring men,
as will be said more fully below;
therefore there has always been the same faith with regard to its substance,
because faith is the bond and, as it were, the form constituting the Church and its members.
Likewise there has always been the same righteousness and holiness in substance
among wayfaring men; but the foundation of righteousness is faith.
Finally, all tend toward the same beatitude, and through the same mediator Christ;
therefore all must agree in the same faith, at least with respect to its substance.

7. First Rule of St. Thomas for Understanding the Proximate Assertion. —
His Conjecture. — Another Conjecture. —
The first rule is that of St. Thomas here, q.1, art. 7, ad 4,
and q. 176, art.6:
that men who were closer to Christ, whether before or after his coming,
had faith more explicit or more perfect than the rest.
St. Thomas supports this rule only by this conjecture:
that in any given man, the state closer to youth is more perfect and robust;
therefore the same holds proportionately in the Church;
but the time of Christ's coming was like the youth of the Church,
for then was the fullness of time in the world,
as Paul says in Galatians 4;
therefore men closer to that age were also more perfect in faith.
We can add another conjecture:
those who are closer to the sun are more illuminated;
but Christ was the true light,
as is said in John 1,
and as long as he was in the world, he was the light of the world,
as he himself also said;
therefore men closer to him were more illuminated by him.

10. Argument against this Second Rule. —
Yet this rule also is not without difficulty,
for nearly the same objections can be raised against it as against the preceding one,
except that many things are assumed in it as uncertain.
What is more difficult is this: that from it it follows that the doctors of the Church who came after the Apostles
were wiser in faith, or had it more explicitly, than the Apostles themselves —
which cannot be said, and is commonly rejected by theologians, even as rash;
for Christ promised especially to those John 14, 15, and 16:
He will teach you all things, and will bring all things to your mind, whatsoever I shall have said to you;
and in the same place he says:
All things whatsoever I have known from my Father, I have made known to you;
and therefore they are said to have the firstfruits of the Spirit, Romans 8,
and 2 Corinthians 3:
But we all, beholding the glory of the Lord with open face;
and Ephesians 1:
According to the riches of his grace, which hath superabounded in us in the wisdom of God;
whence Epiphanius, Heresies 66, near the end, says that
the Apostles received a gift by which the Holy Spirit clearly set forth all things for them,
namely with the clarity of explicit faith, not of science;
and Cyprian, Epistle 74, Augustine, Book 5 On Baptism, ch. 26,
and Cyril, Book 4 on Isaiah, homily 4, hold the same view about the Apostles;
and reason seems to show this, because they were the teachers of the faith of the New Testament,
and therefore the faith of the Church is said to be founded upon the faith of the Apostles;
therefore faith cannot be greater in the Church than it was in the Apostles.

12. First pronouncement for the First Rule: a two-part statement. — First part. — Second part. —
First, then, comparing the state of the Church with respect to its body, or common people,
it is true that there was more explicit faith in the Church in the state or time closer to Christ the Lord.
This is sufficiently known from what has been said and from the discussion made above;
for in the law of nature, faith was more obscure or implicit than under the law of Moses;
and in the law of grace it was much more explicit, both with regard to the mystery of the Trinity —
for, as Gregory above says,
through the New Testament the faith of the Trinity grew in the heart of the faithful people,
in accordance with that text of Matthew, the last chapter:
Go, teach all nations, etc. —
and with regard to the mystery of the Incarnation, concerning which Paul, in Ephesians 3:
In former generations the mystery of Christ was not known,
as it has now been revealed to the holy Apostles and Prophets;
and therefore Irenaeus, Book 3, ch. 47 said:
In the New Testament, the faith of men which is before God has been increased.
But when distinguishing within those same states the times closer to or farther from Christ,
or from the end of the world, I believe nothing certain can be affirmed;
for in each state there could be great variety and change of affairs,
and so in the state of the law of nature faith could have been sufficiently explicit at its beginning,
in keeping with the capacity of that state, and then gradually obscured until the time of Abraham,
in whom it was, as it were, renewed, and perhaps increased in the knowledge of God
as regards his omnipotence, as St. Thomas above says,
and as regards hope in the coming Christ,
on account of the special promise made to Abraham and his children.
But then gradually the knowledge of faith began to diminish,
and so it proceeded until Moses.
Similarly, also in the state of the old law there was great vicissitude and change,
as is evident from Scripture;
and therefore God at certain times sent Prophets,
through whom the knowledge of faith would be, as it were, renewed;
hence it could happen that at the time of the coming of Christ

that people suffered greater ignorance than in earlier times.
Finally, such changes have been found also in the Church of Christ.
But if they are carefully considered, they were more in variety or geographical extension
than in the substantial faith of the body of the Church;
for this always seems to have been equally explicit in the common people of the faithful,
at least as regards the Apostles' Creed.
Nor is this contrary to the rule of St. Thomas,
for this Church is always equally near to Christ:
for he promised both his own presence, saying:
Behold, I am with you until the consummation of the world;
and the assistance of the divine Spirit, saying:
That he may remain with you forever.
